      "text": "We start with the given functional equation:\n\\[\nf(x) + 2f(1 - x) = 3x^2. \\quad \\text{(1)}\n\\]\n\nTo find \\( f(3) \\), we need to determine \\( f(3) \\) in terms of \\( f(x) \\) and \\( f(1 - x) \\). We can do this by substituting specific values for \\( x \\) in the equation (1).\n\nFirst, let's substitute \\( x = 3 \\):\n\\[\nf(3) + 2f(1 - 3) = 3(3)^2.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\nf(3) + 2f(-2) = 27. \\quad \\text{(2)}\n\\]\n\nNext, let's substitute \\( x = -2 \\):\n\\[\nf(-2) + 2f(1 - (-2)) = 3(-2)^2.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\nf(-2) + 2f(3) = 12. \\quad \\text{(3)}\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of linear equations:\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nf(3) + 2f(-2) = 27, \\\\\nf(-2) + 2f(3) = 12.\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\nWe can solve this system by eliminating \\( f(-2) \\). First, multiply equation (3) by 2:\n\\[\n2f(-2) + 4f(3) = 24. \\quad \\text{(4)}\n\\]\n\nNow subtract equation (2) from equation (4):\n\\[\n(2f(-2) + 4f(3)) - (f(3) + 2f(-2)) = 24 - 27.\n\\]\nThis simplifies to:\n\\[\n3f(3) = -3,\n\\]\nso\n\\[\nf(3) = -1.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( f(3) \\) is \\(\\boxed{-1}\\).",
